00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:00.900
And then finally, I'm
going to talk about some

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.820
of the more common
regulatory frameworks

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:05.130
that we've encountered
over the last decade

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.890
in providing G Suite
services to millions

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.840
of customers around the world.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.380
So let's first focus on
compliance assessments.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:15.030
What do you need to
look at when you're

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:19.190
focusing on your business
and the move to the cloud?

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.980
Now, I'm not going
to sit here and talk

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.875
about every regulatory framework
in every country in the world.

00:02:25.875 --> 00:02:27.250
I don't think
anyone can do that.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.900
It would make you crazy
and it would make me crazy.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.390
If you think, there are
190 or so odd countries

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:36.080
with maybe four or five
verticals in each country--

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.690
personal data, healthcare,
educational records,

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.760
financial services, government.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.820
We're talking about hundreds
of thousands or millions

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:44.910
of combinations.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:49.490
So you are the experts in what
your own regulatory compliance

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.012
requirements are for your field.

00:02:52.012 --> 00:02:53.720
You have your own
compliance specialists.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.803
You have your own lawyers.

00:02:54.803 --> 00:02:57.410
And if you don't, presumably
you have a general sense

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.770
of what you've been addressing
over the last few decades

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:05.190
or years that you've
been working with data.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.760
You know where your
regulatory needs are

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.340
and you know what countries
are a priority for you.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.730
However, what we can do as
part of this partnership

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.440
is that we can
bring expertise with

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.020
regards to how
regulatory environments

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.850
would apply to cloud computing.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:23.130
Because we've seen this a lot
over the last several years.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.000
We also have technical expertise
with regards to the services

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.490
we provide and how
they can mesh with what

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:31.100
your own regulatory
compliance needs are.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.870
So there's a partnership.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.090
There's also a shared
responsibility, right?

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:37.310
Because if you're going
to be relying on us,

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.889
really trusting
us with your data,

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:40.430
then you want to
make sure that we're

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.950
doing exactly what we're
supposed to be doing to help

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:44.960
you meet your needs as well.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:48.500
That also means providing
transparency into our services,

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.270
as well as allowing you--

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:53.240
and this is why transparency
is so important--

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.400
to allow you to conduct
a meaningful risk

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:58.430
assessment of our services
so that you can build trust,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.880
and rely on us for your work.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:04.460
So risk assessment--
these are the things

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.430
you should be asking when
you decide to adopt a cloud

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.449
provider, including G Suite.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.240
And I'll show you where
you can find access

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.940
to this type of information
as you conduct your risk

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:15.680
assessment of G Suite.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.810
So obviously, you want to
assess our security capabilities

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.270
and you want to compare them
to what your own security

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.460
capabilities are
right now, as well

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.440
as what might be
expected of you if you

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.110
work in a regulated market.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:33.380
As much as I'd like you to do
this, simply choosing Google

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:35.750
just because we're
Google is not the reason

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.872
to be adopting a cloud provider.

00:04:37.872 --> 00:04:39.830
You want to be able to
assess what our security

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.770
capabilities really are, and
then make a meaningful risk

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:44.340
assessment.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.720
Then you want to think
about ownership of data.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.010
Now, we take the
position that any data

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.560
that you store in G Suite,
that you upload to G Suite,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.850
is your data--

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.640
belongs to you, does
not belong to us.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.991
We own the services
and you own the data.

00:04:58.991 --> 00:05:00.740
And the next thing you
want to think about

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.130
is how is the service
provider using the data.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.930
Now, this might not
be as applicable

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.880
if you're not in a
regulated market.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.850
But in many markets, and in
many regulatory frameworks,

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:13.240
the usage of data is restricted.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:13.970
Right?

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.470
So think about a
European personal data--

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:18.910
if you're a company
and you have employees,

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.770
how that data would
or would not be used.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.850
If you're dealing with
educational records,

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.870
let's say FERPA in the United
States, or student records,

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:29.870
or healthcare information that
might be covered under HIPAA.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.450
You want to think about
data use restrictions.

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:34.850
We'll talk about it more
later, but in G Suite,

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.230
there are no ads.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.000
Data is not used for ads.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.760
And we provide commitments in
our data processing amendment

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.209
that data will be used
for no other purpose

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:44.750
than to provide you
with the services

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.400
and to protect that data.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:48.650
The other thing that's
important we want to talk about

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:50.370
is data incident notification.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.194
That's been a really big
deal over the last few years.

00:05:53.194 --> 00:05:54.860
And people may not
realize it, but there

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:56.870
have been many laws
around the world that

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.510
obligate our
customers as well as

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:02.810
providers to notify
users that might

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:04.940
be affected by a data incident.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.790
Now, notice I don't say breach.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.480
Because it could
be a data leakage,

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.070
it could be an intrusion.

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.680
But in any case, if anything
unfortunate like that happens,

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.380
you may have an obligation
to notify your customers

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:16.750
or your employees.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.710
As such, we as
the cloud provider

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:23.570
obligate ourselves to notify
you if something like that

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:24.320
ever happens.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.360
We hope it never would,
but we need to make sure

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.880
that that is covered.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.700
Data portability-- providing
commitments from your cloud

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.470
provider that you
can take your data

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:35.780
and go home if you want
to switch providers,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.580
or just don't like
their services anymore.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.345
You want to consider that.

00:06:39.345 --> 00:06:40.970
The other thing to
consider-- and this,

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.430
again, comes to particularly
regulated markets--

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.590
is what type of data
you're going to be storing.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.370
For example, you might be
thinking about a government

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.220
agency that deals with
all types of information,

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.590
and classified information.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:56.030
We wouldn't
encourage necessarily

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.080
to have a customer
storing classified data

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.630
in a public cloud
on our systems,

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:02.360
notwithstanding the fact that
we have fantastic security

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.740
capabilities.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:07.460
However, a lot of other data
that they may be addressing--

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.910
collaboration data, calendaring,
document sharing, email--

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:14.254
could very well be uploaded
and used in the cloud.

00:07:14.254 --> 00:07:16.670
So what I'm saying is you need
to look at the type of data

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:18.753
you're dealing with and
decide what is appropriate

00:07:18.753 --> 00:07:21.090
for the cloud, per your
assessment, and what isn't.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:24.380
And we can provide information,
again, regarding our services.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.110
I should also note that
in many, many situations,

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.006
moving your data to
the cloud from on prem

00:07:29.006 --> 00:07:31.130
would end up being much,
much more secure than what

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.510
you could possibly do.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:33.170
Right?

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:34.490
You've got to realize that.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:37.580
So you have to weigh the fact
that the data is likely much

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.220
more secure in the cloud
versus the type of data

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:41.600
you would actually be storing.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.240
And then hopefully, if there's
a nexus between the two,

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.634
that's what you
deploy in the cloud.

00:07:46.634 --> 00:07:48.050
Millions and
millions of customers

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.390
have conducted similar
assessments, I would say.

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:53.330
And that's why we have such
a great system right now

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.250
within G Suite.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:58.010
Finally, you want to consider
where is the data stored.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.040
Google is a global
provider of cloud services,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.579
so we have data
centers globally.

00:08:02.579 --> 00:08:04.370
You need to know where
that information is.

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.530
You need to know where
those data centers are.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:09.290
So again, you can conduct
meaningful risk assessment.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:11.540
I'll talk a little bit about
data centers and the risk

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:14.660
assessment that we conduct
when we choose a data center

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.250
location.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.680
And then finally, you want
to think about third party

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:22.025
security audits with
regards to whether or not

00:08:22.025 --> 00:08:24.650
we're inviting third parties to
review our systems to make sure

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.020
we're doing what
we say we're doing.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.360
These are examples of
questions that you should

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:31.484
be asking of a cloud
provider-- questions

00:08:31.484 --> 00:08:32.900
that we've been
developing answers

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:37.039
to over the course of many years
since we've been doing this.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.590
The first distinction
I absolutely must make,

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:42.350
and I have to dedicate some
time to it specifically here,

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:47.290
is to distinguish G
Suite and consumer apps.

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.250
G Suite and the consumer
app counterparts

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:50.910
look very, very similar.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:53.060
And in fact, the
similarities is somewhat

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:55.100
of a double edged
sword when it comes

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.810
to Google and our customers.

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:59.090
The similarity is
fantastic, because it

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:02.720
makes it a lot easier for
end users, for example--

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.540
when I say end users, I
mean your own employees

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.920
or students--

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:09.260
end users are familiar
with the consumer services,

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.110
it makes the utilization of the
similar services in the work

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:13.939
environment very easy.

00:09:13.939 --> 00:09:15.605
If they know how to
use consumer G-Mail,

00:09:15.605 --> 00:09:17.688
it's very likely they know
how to use the business

00:09:17.688 --> 00:09:19.670
counterpart within G Suite.

00:09:19.670 --> 00:09:23.060
The same goes for Docs
in the business sense.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.442
And you get the idea.

00:09:25.442 --> 00:09:27.900
However, there is a significant
difference between the two,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:28.858
as I mentioned earlier.

00:09:28.858 --> 00:09:32.270
First of all, data is
not used to create ads

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:33.640
or to display ads.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:34.154
Right?

00:09:34.154 --> 00:09:35.570
We provide commitments
in our data

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:38.750
processing amendment-- data
processing terms that restrict

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:40.350
how we can use your data.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.400
See, that's important
for regulated markets.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.550
The other difference is that
G Suite provides customers,

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:48.440
and specifically
their administrators,

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.510
with an administrator console--

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:52.370
a C Panel, a control panel.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.950
And the administrators are
responsible for determining

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:58.100
who in their organization
gets accounts,

00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:00.680
whether their passwords
should be reset, password

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:04.250
enforceability, whether or not
their account should be turned

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.820
off, what systems or what
applications they're allowed

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:10.310
to use, how much data
they have, whether they're

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:13.380
allowed to share outside
the organization.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.350
Our customers are
responsible for doing this.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.050
They are in control.

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:18.020
All right?

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.600
We provide the back end
systems that allow for this,

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.550
but they are the administrators.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:24.890
Compare that to consumer
G-Mail account where there

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.930
is no system administrator.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.770
You simply go to Google
and create your own account

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.100
and decide what you want
to do with it on your own.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.140
The business environment and
the enterprise environment

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:37.295
is very different.

00:10:37.295 --> 00:10:39.170
And then finally, in
the enterprise context--

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:40.750
and I just want to touch on it--

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.859
we also provide special
enterprise related

00:10:42.859 --> 00:10:44.900
functionality, such as
what's available in Google

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:48.140
Vault, which allows for
special retention with

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:49.670
regards to the
data that your end

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:51.720
users may store in the system.

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:54.220
But again, I need to make
the distinction-- in G Suite,

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.810
we will not serve
ads and data won't

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:59.660
be used for any other
purpose than to provide

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:02.430
the services as outlined in
our data processing terms.

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:05.490
And I'll talk about
that a little bit later.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:08.360
So that's the compliance
assessment-- the questions

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.860
you want to ask,
the things you want

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.830
to consider as you
adopt a cloud provider.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.477
However, everything
I could be saying

00:11:18.477 --> 00:11:20.060
to you about what
our capabilities are

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.560
could be wrong.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.827
I could be standing
here just lying to you

00:11:23.827 --> 00:11:25.160
about what our capabilities are.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.993
What everyone has been
telling you all week,

00:11:26.993 --> 00:11:28.850
they could all be lies
and lies and lies.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.650
So how do you know
that they're not lies?

00:11:34.650 --> 00:11:37.297
You have to be able to verify
what we're saying is true,

00:11:37.297 --> 00:11:39.380
at least from a regulatory
compliance perspective.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:41.550
I've worked with
regulators for a long time,

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.740
specifically about cloud.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.652
And they're certainly adopting
the idea of cloud computing.

00:11:46.652 --> 00:11:48.360
Many regulatory
authorities have actually

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.490
issued guidance with
respect to cloud.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:53.460
But they still expect customers
to conduct a meaningful risk

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:54.716
assessment.

00:11:54.716 --> 00:11:56.340
And you can't conduct
a risk assessment

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.100
unless there's some sort of
transparency into what we're

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.271
doing.

00:12:01.271 --> 00:12:03.520
And that's where third party
certifications and audits

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:04.120
come in.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.970
It's very important for this.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:07.720
This is not something
we did when we first

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.500
launched, back in the day,
G-Mail for your domain,

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:15.250
or Google Apps, as we used
to previously call G Suite.

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:18.610
But we realized as larger
and larger customers adopted

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:20.620
our services,
adopted G Suite, they

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.270
wanted to know whether
we were actually

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:25.120
doing what we say we're doing.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:27.120
And how do we do that
with millions and millions

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.920
of customers?

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.507
Well, we didn't have
to invent this process.

00:12:31.507 --> 00:12:33.090
There are third party
auditors that we

00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:36.900
can invite into our systems,
into our data centers,

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:39.660
to review our processes
and analyze what we're

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.270
doing specifically, and
verify what we're doing

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:45.210
is actually what
we say we're doing.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:48.490
So up here, you see a lot of
pretty little badges and logos.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:50.834
I'll give you a little
summary of what they mean.

00:12:50.834 --> 00:12:53.250
The first thing that's very
important in conducting a risk

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.920
assessment is an analysis
of the SOC2 report

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.630
that an auditor might provide.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:13:01.050
Now, basically what we do when
it comes to a SOC examination--

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:03.630
and I'm going to cover
this very high level,

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:05.450
because that's the
best I can do--

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:07.290
is we invite third
party auditors

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.810
to review our processes, our
systems, our data centers,

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:12.450
and to verify that the
controls that we implement

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:14.610
are actually being followed.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:17.430
And the output of their
analysis is a very detailed,

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.620
what we call a
SOC2 audit report.

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.320
Several hundred pages--
200 or 300 pages long--

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:23.880
that give you a
detailed description

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:25.120
of what's happening.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:29.400
We also offer a SOC3, which
is also a synopsis, basically,

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.980
of that review.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.850
The SOC2 report is highly
confidential, very detailed,

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:38.040
but quite useful when it
comes to conducting a risk

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:38.934
assessment.

00:13:38.934 --> 00:13:40.350
So what you want
to do is you want

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:44.260
to look at that detailed SOC2
audit report, or even the SOC3,

00:13:44.260 --> 00:13:46.500
which is publicly available,
and then compare that

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:48.750
to what your own
security capabilities are

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.130
or security requirements.

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:53.940
And then you conduct an analysis
or a comparison between the two

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:56.100
to ensure that there
aren't any gaps

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.690
that you might need to address.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:01.930
The other thing that you
want to think about--

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:03.150
so that's a SOC2.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:03.850
Right?

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:05.980
It's a review of
controls that we've

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.560
implemented by a
third party auditor

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:12.076
to verify that what
we're saying is true.

00:14:12.076 --> 00:14:14.200
Now we have something which
is slightly different--

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:15.070
ISO.

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:18.760
International Organization
for Standardization--

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:19.840
ISO.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.930
ISO 27001 17 and 18.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:24.310
This is where we
have third party

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:29.310
auditors come in and audit us
against a specific standard.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:33.330
ISO, as many of you might
know, is a global organization

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:35.340
that sets standards.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.186
They'll set a security standard.

00:14:37.186 --> 00:14:38.810
They'll set a data
protection standard.

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:41.081
They set standards in
all sorts of industries.

00:14:41.081 --> 00:14:43.080
But the thing that's
become very important to us

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.720
as a provider of cloud services
are their security standards.

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:49.570
So what we've done is we've--
and this was a few years later,

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:53.160
after we started doing SOC
related reports and SAS 70

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:56.700
reports prior to that-- is we'd
invite auditors in to assess us

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:58.650
against this standard.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:02.880
ISO says that if you want
to comply with 27001,

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.170
you have to do these things.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:06.900
And if you do these
things, and if they

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:09.270
are verified by an
auditor, then you

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.510
get a certification,
a diploma basically.

00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:18.576
So for several years now, we've
been audited against ISO 27001.

00:15:18.576 --> 00:15:20.700
And if you're curious about
what those controls are

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.491
that were audited
against, you can actually

00:15:22.491 --> 00:15:24.450
contact ISO and order
a copy of the standard

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:26.910
so you can see what
we're complying with.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.580
More recently--
and as I mentioned,

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:31.860
ISO 27001 is a
security standard.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:36.930
More recently, ISO
has issued 27017,

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:41.400
which are controls that are
specific to Cloud security.

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:42.730
Cloud security.

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:48.040
And naturally, we've
adopted ISO 27017

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:50.570
and have been audited
against that as well.

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:53.620
And then ISO 27018 is
a separate standard

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.660
that is geared towards data
protection and privacy.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.200
It's a privacy
standard for the cloud.

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:01.990
So we've also adopted this,
reviewed the controls,

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:05.170
and had auditors examine us
with respect to the requirements

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:07.900
of ISO 27018.

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:10.060
Then also at the
bottom, these are

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:13.240
industry and region specific
certifications or frameworks.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:15.340
We're also FedRAMP
certified, which

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:17.380
is a US government
certification that

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:21.040
allows us to provide services
to certain government agencies.

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:25.210
We also accept HIPAA data, which
is United States healthcare

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:27.830
information, protected
health information.

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:30.070
And then we also participate
in Privacy Shield

00:16:30.070 --> 00:16:31.210
and model clauses.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:33.550
I'll get to that in
a couple of slides.

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:36.790
The point of this is, though,
we undergo these third party

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:37.900
audits for a reason.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:40.900
It's very expensive and
it takes a long time.

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:44.230
It's so that you have a means
of verifying what we're saying,

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:46.930
and verifying that what we say
we do is actually what we do.

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:52.530
Just to give you a sense
of-- and this is really just

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:55.530
a high level summary-- of the
type of objectives and controls

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:59.850
that would be reviewed
when we subject ourselves

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:01.730
to an ISO type audit.

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:03.840
Obviously, we talk about
information security

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:06.030
and privacy
compliance, but it also

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:08.540
talks about things
like human resources

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:12.480
or the scope of the standard,
what we do with respect

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:14.250
to incident management.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:16.480
Remember when I talked
about if there's-- hopefully

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:17.760
there's never going
to be the case--

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.135
but if there's a data incident,
being able to provide you

00:17:20.135 --> 00:17:22.920
information about it
and in a timely manner.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:24.690
That's part of one
of these standards.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:26.790
Cryptography-- whether
or not we're encrypting

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:29.010
the data and in what manner.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:30.150
The scope of processing.

00:17:30.150 --> 00:17:32.490
You remember, I mentioned
that we obligate ourselves

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:35.460
to limit how data is processed
pursuant to our enterprise data

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.050
processing amendment?

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:39.606
That's part of the
standard as well.

00:17:39.606 --> 00:17:40.980
So if someone asks
you, well, how

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:44.820
do you know Google isn't using
data for advertising purposes,

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:46.580
we can point to the ISO audits.

00:17:46.580 --> 00:17:50.689
And in this case, I
think it's ISO 27018.

00:17:50.689 --> 00:17:51.980
This gives you a general sense.

00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:55.800
But again, as part of
your risk assessment,

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.940
if you really want to
conduct your due diligence,

00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:01.170
then obtain a copy of
these controls from ISO--

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:08.130
ISO 27001 security, ISO
27017 cloud security,

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:11.430
and ISO 27018 cloud privacy--

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:12.930
and review the controls.

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:15.450
Then you'll get a sense of
what we actually audit against

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:17.810
and what our capabilities are.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:21.560
This, along with the
SOC2 report I mentioned--

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:24.410
that very detailed 200
to 300 page summary--

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:27.110
or the SOC3, which is
publicly available ,

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:29.930
will give you a sense of
what our overall security

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:33.012
capabilities are as well as
data protection capabilities,

00:18:33.012 --> 00:18:34.970
and then compare it to
what your own needs are.

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:42.880
There are also lots of
other online resources.

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:44.260
We have FAQs.

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.070
We have white papers.

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:48.100
We have documents that
describe our processes

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:49.899
outside of these audit reports.

00:18:49.899 --> 00:18:51.190
You should take a look at them.

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:54.130
You should avail yourselves
of these resources.

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:57.407
We create these for people that
are in regulated industries.

00:18:57.407 --> 00:18:58.990
But it doesn't hurt,
even if you don't

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.323
think you're dealing with
regulated information-- you're

00:19:01.323 --> 00:19:03.960
not a bank, you're not dealing
with European personal data,

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.220
or student records, or
educational records.

00:19:06.220 --> 00:19:06.847
That's OK.

00:19:06.847 --> 00:19:08.680
You can take a look at
these and get a sense

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.150
of what we're actually doing.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:12.010
It gives information
about how we encrypt

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:15.790
the data, general overviews
about security and compliance,

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:18.640
our data centers, we
have security frequently

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:21.760
asked questions.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:25.200
So the next thing I want to
talk about are data centers.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:26.860
As I mentioned, we have--

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:28.720
and this is publicly
available, by the way.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:30.470
If you do a search for
Google data center,

00:19:30.470 --> 00:19:32.460
you'll be able to
find this page.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:34.270
But this is a general
overview of where

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:37.540
we have production data centers
that Google owns and operates

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:41.267
that we use for systems
that would involve G Suite.

00:19:41.267 --> 00:19:42.850
And I want to talk
to you a little bit

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:45.340
about why we have chosen
these particular data centers

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:49.397
and why these locations
are important to us.

00:19:49.397 --> 00:19:51.730
So first of all, there are
considerations that you might

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:54.650
not be surprised to learn.

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:57.550
For example, bandwidth is
obviously a big issue for us.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:19:59.860
We want to make sure that
the data centers enjoy

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:02.230
fast internet
connectivity, which

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:07.224
results in extremely low latency
at reasonable prices to us.

00:20:07.224 --> 00:20:08.890
And we also want to
make sure that there

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:12.550
are multiple and diverse
fiber paths to the data center

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:14.500
where we might choose
to have it built.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:18.160
And notice, we have
distributed data centers

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:19.790
based on that criteria.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.570
Cost-- as I alluded to a
little bit earlier-- cost

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:24.800
is also very important.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.980
We want to make sure
that the location would

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:30.490
allow for energy efficiency
and provide, if possible,

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:32.950
renewable energy options.

00:20:32.950 --> 00:20:36.190
These are all engineering
driven considerations.

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:37.930
But from a compliance
perspective,

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:39.600
it goes a little bit
further than that.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:40.330
Right?

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:43.090
We also want to
consider policy issues.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.030
For example, we don't want
to build a data center

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:49.360
in a place that does not have
political stability, that

00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.590
does not have a rule of law.

00:20:51.590 --> 00:20:53.590
We don't want to see a
data center nationalized,

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:55.940
for example.

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:59.530
We want to make sure that
the countries we might choose

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:05.800
has a good human rights record,
or is a proponent of democracy,

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:09.570
does not censor
political considerations.

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:10.150
All right.

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:12.670
We have to be able to defend
our rights and the rights

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:15.130
of our users when we build
a data center in a country.

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:17.470
And that's why rule
of law is critical.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:20.415
So for example, where we
build a data center might also

00:21:20.415 --> 00:21:22.540
determine, for example,
whether we have the ability

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:24.970
to protect ourselves
and our users in courts

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:27.760
by the mere fact we have
a data center there.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.094
It goes beyond that, also.

00:21:29.094 --> 00:21:30.760
We want to make sure
that content is not

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:32.220
going to be a problem.

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:33.970
The country that we
build a data center in

00:21:33.970 --> 00:21:35.980
should allow us to be
able to protect ourselves

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:40.870
against copyright infringement
claims or defamation

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:42.040
or trademark.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:43.150
Right?

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:45.640
So we have to consider
any exposure there.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:48.100
And finally, you won't see
data centers in any countries

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:51.520
where we, as a cloud provider,
aren't allowed to do business

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:53.460
with people in that country.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:53.960
Right?

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:55.970
There are countries
that we are prohibited

00:21:55.970 --> 00:21:58.640
from even considering or
doing business in that might

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:00.130
be on the OFAC restricted list.

00:22:02.612 --> 00:22:03.820
These are the considerations.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:07.140
And again, you should know
where the data is stored as part

00:22:07.140 --> 00:22:08.560
of your risk assessment.

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:11.340
And I should also let you
know that since we do utilize

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:14.190
a global distributed
network system,

00:22:14.190 --> 00:22:16.590
you should also consider
that when you determine

00:22:16.590 --> 00:22:18.210
the type of data you store.

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:19.180
All right.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:21.672
But you should also remember
that it's quite possible

00:22:21.672 --> 00:22:24.130
that when you think there are
restrictions to, for example,

00:22:24.130 --> 00:22:26.005
things that might relate
to data sovereignty,

00:22:26.005 --> 00:22:27.730
they might not
actually be the case.

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:31.000
For example, when it comes
to European personal data,

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.600
we actually provide
and undertake

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.930
to utilize frameworks that allow
the export of personal data

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:37.500
from Europe.

00:22:37.500 --> 00:22:39.940
A lot of people don't know
that, but it can be done,

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:41.212
and it's done quite readily.

00:22:41.212 --> 00:22:43.170
I'll talk a little bit
more about that as well.

00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:50.270
So we talked about
transparency, right?

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:51.590
Third party audit reports--

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:55.580
SOC2, ISO certifications,
white papers,

00:22:55.580 --> 00:22:59.242
publicly available
information, FAQs.

00:22:59.242 --> 00:23:00.950
That's how you can do
your due diligence.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:05.330
But how do you know that
as soon as you sign up,

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:07.050
we don't do any of it?

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:07.590
Right?

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:08.714
Well, we changed our minds.

00:23:08.714 --> 00:23:10.830
We don't want to do third
party audits, right?

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:11.550
Too much trouble.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:12.910
Too expensive.

00:23:12.910 --> 00:23:14.862
You have to have it in writing.

00:23:14.862 --> 00:23:16.320
That's where the
contracts come in.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:19.290
And this is where I can show
you where we put it in writing

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:22.010
and where you can find it.

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:27.380
So we basically offer
three different means

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.870
by which we provide
these obligations to you.

00:23:29.870 --> 00:23:32.660
First of all, there is, of
course, the customer agreement.

00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:34.868
This is what you would click
through when you sign up

00:23:34.868 --> 00:23:36.770
for G Suite for business
or for education,

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:38.780
or our other
versions of G Suite.

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:40.460
It provides your
commercial terms--

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:43.490
how much you're going
to pay and for what,

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:45.050
billing and payments
information,

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:47.870
things like technical
support, SLAs.

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:50.570
It also talks about your
data remains yours--

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:51.670
intellectual property.

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:54.003
Remember I mentioned that as
one of the risks assessment

00:23:54.003 --> 00:23:54.784
questions?

00:23:54.784 --> 00:23:57.200
Who owns the data when you
upload it into a cloud provider

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:57.700
systems?

00:23:57.700 --> 00:23:58.470
You do.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:23:59.720
That's where we talk about it.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:05.680
But my favorite part, which
I talk about with respect

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:08.290
to data protection and
regulatory compliance,

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:10.090
are the data processing terms.

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:13.660
We actually have an amendment
that anyone that uses G Suite

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.582
can opt into through
the Admin console--

00:24:15.582 --> 00:24:17.290
remember, I mentioned
the admin council--

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:20.380
that provides for the data
protection obligations

00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:23.860
that we impose on ourselves
as your cloud provider.

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:26.290
We talk about
privacy compliance.

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:29.380
We talk about the
restriction on data use.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:31.180
We maintain the obligation--

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:34.840
or rather we impose the
obligation on ourselves

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:36.820
to maintain these
third party audits,

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:38.832
which have to be
done periodically.

00:24:38.832 --> 00:24:40.540
We talk about things
like data deletion--

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:44.614
making sure that when you delete
a file, it's actually deleted.

00:24:44.614 --> 00:24:46.030
Again, we think
about these things

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:48.250
because it comes
up over the years.

00:24:48.250 --> 00:24:51.160
You can leverage our
familiarity with this process

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:53.084
in regulatory environment.

00:24:53.084 --> 00:24:55.000
And then, of course, not
everyone in the world

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:56.650
is the same.

00:24:56.650 --> 00:25:00.130
We leverage global standards for
our third party audit reports,

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:01.960
because we want it to
be able to encompass

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.000
as many customers as possible.

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:08.680
So we don't block off or fail
to serve a certain segment

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:10.210
of the market.

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:12.670
But we also provide region
or industry specific

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:16.462
terms based on the size of the
market and how much of a need.

00:25:16.462 --> 00:25:18.670
And I'll talk about model
clauses a little bit later,

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:22.570
but that's something specific
to European privacy compliance.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:24.340
What allows the export
of personal data

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:27.350
to a global network
of data centers?

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:29.650
We provide business
associates agreements,

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:32.830
which is contractual
assurances to our users,

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:36.060
or to our customers, that need
to process HIPAA information--

00:25:36.060 --> 00:25:40.010
PHI, patient information
that is protected.

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:43.510
We also provide FSI,
financial services industry--

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.990
special audit language that some
Googlers share in the room that

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:49.330
have worked very hard
to help us create,

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:51.820
which will address the specific
regulatory requirements

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:55.256
of our customers in the
financial services industry.

00:25:55.256 --> 00:25:56.630
And then finally,
as I mentioned,

00:25:56.630 --> 00:26:00.160
we provide clauses related
to our FedRAMP certification,

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:02.209
which is the US
certification that

00:26:02.209 --> 00:26:04.000
allow certain government
agencies to use us

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.152
for moderate level data.

00:26:06.152 --> 00:26:07.610
So this is generally
the construct.

00:26:07.610 --> 00:26:10.540
We have the base
customer agreement,

00:26:10.540 --> 00:26:13.086
then you have specific
data processing terms,

00:26:13.086 --> 00:26:14.960
and then you have region
or industry specific

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:18.380
terms that might be available
for you, depending on where

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:21.300
you're coming to us from.

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:24.300
Let's drill down into the
data processing terms.

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:28.200
So the data processing
terms are, in addition

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:30.090
to our products'
function, is really

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:33.090
what makes the enterprise
service stand out

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:34.860
from the consumer service.

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:37.740
The consumer service, the
consumer privacy policy.

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:39.900
This is where you have
the business oriented data

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:42.060
protection obligations.

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:43.740
It's essentially
like the privacy

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:48.360
policy for the enterprise
service that we're providing.

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:51.090
As I mentioned, it's available
via the admin console.

00:26:51.090 --> 00:26:53.580
And it was created with
feedback over the years

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:56.160
from customers and
regulatory authorities alike.

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:58.680
We work with everybody
to get feedback

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:02.430
on this globally available
data processing amendment,

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:03.150
we call it.

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.370
It's available to
anyone in the world.

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:08.160
And all you need
to do is opt in.

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:11.370
And very important.

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:13.350
But let's talk a little
bit about, again,

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:15.000
what some of the
obligations are.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.070
And you'll see
some of these pair

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:18.540
directly to what
I was mentioning

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:21.480
as part of the initial risk
assessment of a cloud provider,

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:25.260
as well as things we talked
about with relation to audits.

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:28.110
We include a limitation
on processing.

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:30.330
We include a restriction
on using the data for ads

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:31.860
or for serving ads.

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.110
We obligate ourselves to
actually delete the data

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:35.190
when you delete it.

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:38.430
In other words, if
you take an email

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:39.930
and you delete it
from the inbox,

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:43.517
and then you empty your trash,
we provide an obligation

00:27:43.517 --> 00:27:45.600
that we will wipe the data
within a certain period

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:46.980
of time.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.380
And similar to the
ISO certifications,

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.290
and the number of security
engineers we have,

00:27:51.290 --> 00:27:52.860
and our security
capabilities, this

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:56.334
is likely a significant step
up from what people do on prem.

00:27:56.334 --> 00:27:58.500
I don't know a lot of people
that actually wipe data

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:01.140
from their servers every time
a piece of data is deleted.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:05.830
You may do it, but I'll
bet that many folks don't.

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:09.190
We maintain an obligation,
as I mentioned earlier,

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:12.280
to have these third party
auditors come in regularly

00:28:12.280 --> 00:28:15.640
and create these audit reports
so you can review them.

00:28:15.640 --> 00:28:18.190
In addition to making sure we
maintain these audit reports

00:28:18.190 --> 00:28:20.023
throughout the contract,
throughout the term

00:28:20.023 --> 00:28:21.880
of the contract, we
make sure we maintain

00:28:21.880 --> 00:28:25.580
our certifications-- the ISO
certifications, for example.

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:29.786
We also tell you-- and this is
something we've done with close

00:28:29.786 --> 00:28:31.660
consultation with the
regulatory authorities,

00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:32.860
particularly in Europe--

00:28:32.860 --> 00:28:36.860
is we provide information to
you about our subprocessors.

00:28:36.860 --> 00:28:39.160
So what are subprocessors?

00:28:39.160 --> 00:28:41.950
These are third parties,
non-Google Inc. entities

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:44.330
that we would use
to process the data.

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:47.890
So for example, Google has
engineers all over the place.

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:50.675
And we have data centers.

00:28:50.675 --> 00:28:52.300
So this basically is
information to you

00:28:52.300 --> 00:28:56.470
about our own subsidiaries
that process data for you.

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:58.270
Google companies
themselves that would

00:28:58.270 --> 00:28:59.980
be involved with processing.

00:28:59.980 --> 00:29:02.290
We also have information
about the number

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:04.852
of vendors we use that
would process data as well.

00:29:04.852 --> 00:29:06.310
Now, as you can
imagine, as Google,

00:29:06.310 --> 00:29:09.107
we don't use many vendors
for processing data.

00:29:09.107 --> 00:29:10.940
I think, right now, the
last time I checked,

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.340
we had less than 10.

00:29:12.340 --> 00:29:14.465
But still, it's good
to know who they are.

00:29:14.465 --> 00:29:16.090
And we make it
available to you as part

00:29:16.090 --> 00:29:17.980
of your risk assessment.

00:29:17.980 --> 00:29:21.280
We also provide an obligation
to notify you, for example,

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:24.247
with regards to these vendors if
the vendor is going to change,

00:29:24.247 --> 00:29:25.330
so you know ahead of time.

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:29.560
Just in case if it's
of interest to you.

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:31.705
Again, transparency--
an obligation

00:29:31.705 --> 00:29:33.860
to provide you with
information so you can conduct

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:36.130
a meaningful risk assessment.

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:39.070
By the way, I should tell you
that a lot of these obligations

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:40.930
come out of Europe.

00:29:40.930 --> 00:29:43.000
For our data
protection framework,

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:45.280
data processing amendment,
data processing terms,

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:47.920
and as you'll learn
later, a lot of our work

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:50.140
is based on the European model.

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:53.080
Because from a data protection
and personal data standpoint,

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:55.660
they offer some of the most
stringent data protection

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:57.490
requirements in the world.

00:29:57.490 --> 00:29:59.680
So we hope that if we
achieve this standard,

00:29:59.680 --> 00:30:03.670
it will be useful to the rest of
the world to a certain degree.

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:06.160
Now, of course, everyone
has their own requirements.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:09.530
Every country has their own
requests, their own needs.

00:30:09.530 --> 00:30:11.520
But by using a high
standard like Europe

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:15.490
for data protection, as
well as using globally

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:20.170
accepted third party auditing
mechanisms and certifications,

00:30:20.170 --> 00:30:21.970
we can create a
framework that is

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:25.630
applicable to as many customers
worldwide as possible.

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:28.480
So we also talk about
our security measures.

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:32.170
We give you an overview of
the security capabilities.

00:30:32.170 --> 00:30:33.940
We provide an
obligation to notify you

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:35.361
in the event of a data incident.

00:30:35.361 --> 00:30:35.860
All right.

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:36.359
Remember?

00:30:36.359 --> 00:30:37.600
I keep talking about that.

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:40.460
This is where we promise
to do in writing.

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:42.840
And then finally, if there
are further questions

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:44.440
that you need
further information,

00:30:44.440 --> 00:30:47.200
we provide a link to be able
to contact the data privacy

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:50.256
officer to be able to
get more information.

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:55.057
So that's contracts.

00:30:55.057 --> 00:30:56.140
I didn't make it too long.

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:58.280
I didn't want to
make anybody crazy.

00:30:58.280 --> 00:31:00.850
Besides, there are hardly any
lawyers in the room, or no one

00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:01.975
that will admit it, anyway.

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:05.720
Regulatory frameworks.

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:08.120
Let's talk about that.

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:11.230
So I mentioned that
we try to provide

00:31:11.230 --> 00:31:13.450
a service that is useful
to as many customers

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:15.166
worldwide as possible.

00:31:15.166 --> 00:31:17.290
But I've also said that we
have a lot of experience

00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:20.230
with dealing with regulatory
authorities in markets

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:21.834
that we've served
over the world.

00:31:21.834 --> 00:31:24.250
I want to talk to you a little
about some of these markets

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:26.740
and how we have
addressed them directly,

00:31:26.740 --> 00:31:29.590
and also what we've seen
from a trend's perspective.

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:32.920
So naturally, the first thing
I'm going to talk about is EU.

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:36.640
You've heard me mention that EU
data protection frameworks are

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:38.770
particularly stringent,
and it's something

00:31:38.770 --> 00:31:41.290
that we work very hard
to address always.

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:44.050
And we intend to do so.

00:31:44.050 --> 00:31:48.430
So let's talk a little bit
about EU privacy compliance.

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:50.800
And I'm going to start
this story with the EU Data

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:53.650
Protection Directive of 1995.

00:31:53.650 --> 00:31:56.350
This was a very important piece
of legislation that basically

00:31:56.350 --> 00:31:59.710
directed all the
member states of Europe

00:31:59.710 --> 00:32:02.845
to pass their own privacy laws
that mirrored this directive.

00:32:02.845 --> 00:32:04.220
That's what's
called a directive,

00:32:04.220 --> 00:32:07.330
it directs the member
states to do that.

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:10.270
One of the most important
aspects of the Data Protection

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:12.460
Directive, besides
the protection

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:16.000
of personal data, data
controller, data processor

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:18.340
relationship, the
obligations of the parties--

00:32:18.340 --> 00:32:20.870
I'm not going to get into
too much detail on that.

00:32:20.870 --> 00:32:24.610
But the prohibition on exporting
personal data from Europe

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:27.550
to any country that did not
maintain adequate privacy

00:32:27.550 --> 00:32:32.560
protections as
determined by Europe.

00:32:32.560 --> 00:32:34.780
Well, when the Data
Protection Directive

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:36.895
was being drafted
prior to 1995, this

00:32:36.895 --> 00:32:38.770
was also prior to when
the internet was being

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:41.260
used for commerce generally.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:43.960
And that's totally different.

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:45.220
Huge shift.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:47.440
Now you've got personal
data flying everywhere--

00:32:47.440 --> 00:32:50.770
email address, a name,
a picture, e-mailing

00:32:50.770 --> 00:32:52.640
from one person to another.

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:55.750
It made the aspect of locating
personal data in a region where

00:32:55.750 --> 00:32:58.832
it could be enforced
particularly difficult. Well,

00:32:58.832 --> 00:33:01.040
the first thing that happened
with relation to that--

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:03.970
and I'm going to talk about this
from the cloud perspective--

00:33:03.970 --> 00:33:07.780
is that the European Commission
created the US/EU Safe Harbor

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:09.020
Framework.

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:11.820
Who here has heard
of Safe Harbor?

00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:12.322
Wow.

00:33:12.322 --> 00:33:14.280
See, a lot has happened
in the news with regard

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:15.300
to Safe Harbor.

00:33:15.300 --> 00:33:18.300
Safe Harbor was a special
framework that was created

00:33:18.300 --> 00:33:20.640
between the United States
Department of Commerce

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:24.090
and the European Commission
that would allow the flow

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:27.480
of personal data from Europe
to the United States--

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:30.090
to companies in the United
States, I should say--

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:34.500
that opt to participate in
the Safe Harbor Program.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:36.450
And thousands and
thousands of companies

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:38.580
signed up to do this,
including Google.

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:42.930
We imported data pursuant to
the Safe Harbor Framework.

00:33:42.930 --> 00:33:46.020
But that covered the United
States for cloud providers.

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:49.410
In 2010, the European
Commission created a framework

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.600
that would allow the
export of personal data

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:53.580
to anywhere in the world.

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:57.350
And that was a mechanism they
called model Contract clauses.

00:33:57.350 --> 00:33:59.490
I won't go into too much
detail, but basically it

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:01.590
was foreign language
that they created

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:04.740
where if this language
exists in the contract

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:09.100
between the customer and, in
our case, the cloud provider,

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:11.699
then data could flow
anywhere in the world

00:34:11.699 --> 00:34:14.066
pursuant to the requirements
in the model clauses.

00:34:14.066 --> 00:34:16.440
Now, it's not just a question
of a cloud provider copying

00:34:16.440 --> 00:34:19.210
and pasting this language,
this boilerplate language

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:21.030
into a contract.

00:34:21.030 --> 00:34:22.739
The obligations in
the model clauses

00:34:22.739 --> 00:34:27.179
are significant and serious
and expensive and difficult

00:34:27.179 --> 00:34:28.230
for us to do.

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:31.210
But we did it, and we do it.

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:33.210
And we began offering
model clauses

00:34:33.210 --> 00:34:37.020
somewhere around
December of 2012.

00:34:37.020 --> 00:34:39.389
So we offered
customers Safe Harbor,

00:34:39.389 --> 00:34:41.100
which was built into
all of our services,

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:43.949
and frankly, used
all across Google.

00:34:43.949 --> 00:34:47.969
And then for G Suite, we offered
model clauses as another way

00:34:47.969 --> 00:34:49.620
to export personal
data from Europe.

00:34:49.620 --> 00:34:51.900
Sort of a parallel
framework, both.

00:34:51.900 --> 00:34:54.750
And we started
doing that in 2012.

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:58.160
And then what happened most
recently with Safe Harbor?

00:34:58.160 --> 00:34:59.300
Gone.

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:03.290
It was ruled invalid by
Europe's highest court.

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:05.960
Now, in our case,
of course, we never

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:07.970
could have anticipated
that that would

00:35:07.970 --> 00:35:09.560
have happened with Safe Harbor.

00:35:09.560 --> 00:35:12.470
But we did, thankfully,
offer this parallel framework

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:16.440
for model clauses for several
years before this happened.

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:18.770
So our customers had an
alternative framework

00:35:18.770 --> 00:35:21.530
to do this for this data export.

00:35:21.530 --> 00:35:23.450
This is important,
even if you are not

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:24.920
based in Europe, by the way.

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:28.040
If you are a multinational
company or organization that

00:35:28.040 --> 00:35:31.740
has end users in Europe or users
that are European citizens,

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:34.914
this is also important.

00:35:34.914 --> 00:35:36.830
And then of course, the
Department of Commerce

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:39.350
and the European Commission
created Privacy Shield

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:41.060
as a replacement
for Safe Harbor.

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:43.890
So we also offer
Privacy Shield as well.

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:47.230
So we have model clauses
and we have Privacy Shield.

00:35:47.230 --> 00:35:50.060
This is, again, for the export
of personal data from Europe.

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:51.770
Important for a
multinational data

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:55.130
center or a cloud provider
that uses international data

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:56.040
centers.

00:35:56.040 --> 00:35:58.010
We also have a
contractual obligation--

00:35:58.010 --> 00:36:02.160
which I didn't include in the
data processing terms slide,

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:03.550
but I should talk about it--

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:05.990
where we promise to maintain
an alternative framework

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:08.976
to stay a step ahead as
the law changes with regard

00:36:08.976 --> 00:36:10.100
to export of personal data.

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:15.240
Now, with respect
to model clauses,

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:19.170
I should note that when
Safe Harbor was invalidated,

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:21.600
pretty much all we were
left with for G Suite was

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:24.810
model clauses as a mechanism
to export personal data

00:36:24.810 --> 00:36:28.020
and we had millions and
millions of customers in Europe

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:29.850
or those that had
users in Europe

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:32.470
that we needed to protect

00:36:32.470 --> 00:36:35.530
So being that we have
model clauses specifically,

00:36:35.530 --> 00:36:38.830
we needed to make sure that data
protection regulators actually

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:41.950
agreed that our use
of them addressed

00:36:41.950 --> 00:36:45.110
European requirements for
the export of personal data.

00:36:45.110 --> 00:36:47.200
So along those
lines, we submitted

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:53.440
our contracts, our contractual
agreement, the data processing

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:56.500
amendment I mentioned, as
well as our own model clauses

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:59.050
for review by data
protection regulators

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:02.170
in Europe pursuant
to a framework that

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:06.100
was created by the
Article 29 Working Party.

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:08.050
Now, the Article
29 Working Party

00:37:08.050 --> 00:37:10.670
is basically the group of
all European data protection

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:12.130
regulators.

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:16.450
They created a process by
which a provider's terms could

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:19.840
be reviewed and the model
clauses could be assessed

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:21.520
as to whether or
not they achieve

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:24.670
European requirements for
the export of personal data.

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:28.300
And happy to say that after a
year of hard work, discussions

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:30.850
with the data protection
regulators, review

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:34.180
of our contracts,
changes as necessary,

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:37.660
we obtained approval from the
data protection regulators that

00:37:37.660 --> 00:37:39.190
reviewed our model clauses.

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:40.900
And again, we do
this because it's

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:42.940
very important for
all of our users

00:37:42.940 --> 00:37:45.520
who maintain
businesses in Europe

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:47.410
or have end users in Europe.

00:37:47.410 --> 00:37:52.090
So basically what this means
is that model clauses--

00:37:52.090 --> 00:37:54.666
our customers that are in Europe
that use model clauses don't

00:37:54.666 --> 00:37:57.040
need to get special permission
from their data protection

00:37:57.040 --> 00:37:59.780
regulator to export
personal data.

00:37:59.780 --> 00:38:05.060
And for other countries, it
makes the process much easier.

00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:06.890
This makes it much
easier for our customers

00:38:06.890 --> 00:38:08.870
to conduct a risk assessment.

00:38:08.870 --> 00:38:10.820
If we have any German
privacy lawyers in here,

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:12.741
they would know
exactly what we mean.

00:38:12.741 --> 00:38:14.240
And as I mentioned,
it was conducted

00:38:14.240 --> 00:38:16.220
pursuant to Article
29 Working Party

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:17.990
guidance, the group
of all European data

00:38:17.990 --> 00:38:19.520
protection regulators.

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:23.180
As part of this process, the
lead data protection regulator

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:25.910
who conducted this assessment
was the Irish DPA--

00:38:25.910 --> 00:38:26.954
Ireland.

00:38:26.954 --> 00:38:28.370
And as part of
this process, there

00:38:28.370 --> 00:38:30.420
are two co-reviewers,
two other data protection

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:33.620
regulators that review
this on behalf of Europe.

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:36.560
And the co-reviewers were
the Spanish Data Protection

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:39.830
Authority, who also provided
a lot of great feedback,

00:38:39.830 --> 00:38:43.780
and also the Hamburg, Germany
data protection regulator.

00:38:43.780 --> 00:38:46.760
And for people that operate
in data protection space,

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:49.460
to have a regulator
based in Germany

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:51.840
sign off on model clauses,
that's a big deal.

00:38:51.840 --> 00:38:54.740
And we're very, very
happy to be providing this

00:38:54.740 --> 00:38:57.900
to our customers.

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:00.720
So we can't talk about
Europe without discussing

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:04.260
the General Data Protection
Regulation, the GDPR.

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:06.000
Who here has heard of the GDPR?

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:09.274
That's great.

00:39:09.274 --> 00:39:10.190
I'm glad to hear that.

00:39:10.190 --> 00:39:12.682
And as I tell my colleagues--
the engineering and product

00:39:12.682 --> 00:39:13.640
management colleagues--

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:18.500
GDPR does not mean groovy
disco party revolution.

00:39:18.500 --> 00:39:20.850
I try to explain this to them.

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:25.160
So do you remember in that slide
where I started with the Data

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:29.330
Protection Directive
in 1995, and how

00:39:29.330 --> 00:39:31.640
that was created prior to
the internet being used

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:34.190
for commerce really
and how that created

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:37.880
a lot of interesting
situations for companies

00:39:37.880 --> 00:39:41.540
that export personal data
regularly using the internet,

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:43.310
for example.

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:46.970
So Europe decided to update the
EU Data Protection Directive

00:39:46.970 --> 00:39:49.910
and create a new version
with a lot of changes

00:39:49.910 --> 00:39:54.620
and a lot of improvements, which
will take effect in May 2018.

00:39:54.620 --> 00:39:56.260
And notice, I just
should point out,

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:58.880
that this is going to be called
the General Data Protection

00:39:58.880 --> 00:40:02.800
Regulation, and not a directive.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:04.660
They didn't just do
this to confuse us.

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:07.690
The idea is that the
problem with the directive

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:10.270
was that remember I
mentioned each country passed

00:40:10.270 --> 00:40:12.470
their own version?

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:14.690
Each version in each country
was slightly different

00:40:14.690 --> 00:40:16.950
and made customers
and us kind of crazy.

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:19.130
Cloud providers were getting
a little loopy with it.

00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:21.100
Customers didn't know
how to interpret it.

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:24.770
So to try to create consistency
across all of Europe,

00:40:24.770 --> 00:40:27.545
or the EU, I should say, they
made this a regulation so

00:40:27.545 --> 00:40:30.860
that it has direct force of law.

00:40:30.860 --> 00:40:34.060
Now, what's nice about this from
a cloud provider perspective is

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:36.310
that a lot of the things
that we've already been doing

00:40:36.310 --> 00:40:40.750
for several years pursuant to
EU guidance from the Article 29

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:43.970
Working Party, like what cloud
providers should be doing--

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:46.330
they have Working Party
opinions with regards to that--

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:48.160
data protection,
obligations that

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:50.380
were in the model
clauses I mentioned.

00:40:50.380 --> 00:40:52.900
A lot of those are going to
now be included in the Data

00:40:52.900 --> 00:40:54.550
Protection Regulation.

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:56.320
So from a data
protection perspective,

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:59.000
it's very helpful
for us as a provider.

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:03.040
It also maintains similar
data transfer mechanisms

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:04.390
that I talked about.

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:08.470
Remember I mentioned model
clauses and Privacy Shield?

00:41:08.470 --> 00:41:10.900
Those types of data
export mechanisms

00:41:10.900 --> 00:41:15.137
are going to be consistent and
be maintained within the GDPR.

00:41:15.137 --> 00:41:15.970
They may be tweaked.

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:17.140
They may be updated.

00:41:17.140 --> 00:41:20.850
But the core concept
still remains.

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:23.540
However, there are
things that are new.

00:41:23.540 --> 00:41:26.180
From a data protection
perspective previously

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:29.900
and the way it is today,
customers in Europe,

00:41:29.900 --> 00:41:32.375
they are the ones that are
responsible for compliance

00:41:32.375 --> 00:41:34.250
with the data protection
requirements in each

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:35.550
of their countries.

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:38.210
They are the ones who are
beholden to their regulators.

00:41:38.210 --> 00:41:41.120
Now, we provide all of
these European centric data

00:41:41.120 --> 00:41:43.670
protection commitments
to our customers

00:41:43.670 --> 00:41:46.430
because they need it,
as customers in Europe

00:41:46.430 --> 00:41:49.160
or customers with
European end users,

00:41:49.160 --> 00:41:51.591
and also because
it's important for us

00:41:51.591 --> 00:41:53.090
as a business to
make sure they have

00:41:53.090 --> 00:41:56.990
what they need to be protected
and to use our systems.

00:41:56.990 --> 00:42:00.350
With the General Data
Protection Regulation,

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:03.350
the GDPR, now the
providers are also

00:42:03.350 --> 00:42:06.410
going to be directly responsible
for the data protection

00:42:06.410 --> 00:42:08.310
regulators in Europe.

00:42:08.310 --> 00:42:12.200
So not only are customers, but
the cloud providers as well.

00:42:12.200 --> 00:42:15.420
That gets our attention.

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:17.930
The other thing is that there
is a very strict timeline when

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:21.820
it comes to data incident
or breach notification.

00:42:21.820 --> 00:42:23.610
That's a really big deal.

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:25.460
Now, as I mentioned
for years, we've

00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:28.760
been providing data breach
obligations or data incident

00:42:28.760 --> 00:42:29.840
notification obligations.

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:33.080
But now, they've got a real,
very challenging timeline,

00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:35.390
which has gotten the attention
of the entire industry

00:42:35.390 --> 00:42:37.460
as well as customer base.

00:42:37.460 --> 00:42:40.430
Also, fines--
fines are extremely

00:42:40.430 --> 00:42:42.540
high now for noncompliance.

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:45.440
So that also gets the
attention of the consumers

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:47.120
and the providers as well.

00:42:47.120 --> 00:42:49.160
And there's also an express
requirement for data

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:52.006
portability, which didn't
exist in prior guidance.

00:42:52.006 --> 00:42:53.630
Remember we talked
about the obligation

00:42:53.630 --> 00:42:55.338
to be able to allow
you to take your data

00:42:55.338 --> 00:42:58.220
and go home, move to
a different provider?

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:00.620
That's now actually
in the regulation.

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:04.490
So from a compliance
perspective,

00:43:04.490 --> 00:43:06.330
we're in pretty good shape.

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:10.280
But we are definitely
focusing on the regulation,

00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:14.000
which is going to be
taking effect next year.

00:43:14.000 --> 00:43:16.070
And stay tuned for
more information.

00:43:16.070 --> 00:43:18.590
We're absolutely going to
be updating you with this.

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:23.370
Now, as I mentioned,
this section

00:43:23.370 --> 00:43:26.375
talks about the regulatory
frameworks we see most often.

00:43:26.375 --> 00:43:28.500
And I can't talk about the
millions of combinations

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:29.870
in every country.

00:43:29.870 --> 00:43:33.400
But I can talk about
general key themes as well.

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:35.550
So as I've mentioned
the risk assessment--

00:43:35.550 --> 00:43:37.740
how important that
is and the ability

00:43:37.740 --> 00:43:40.890
to look at provider's
terms and audit reports

00:43:40.890 --> 00:43:42.540
and certifications.

00:43:42.540 --> 00:43:47.190
If I want to talk about a
common theme across industries

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:50.970
and across regulators and
across countries, that's it.

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:53.550
Conducting a meaningful
risk assessment.

00:43:53.550 --> 00:43:56.600
Don't just use Google
because we're Google.

00:43:56.600 --> 00:43:58.440
Right?

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:00.300
I've actually seen
someone get in trouble

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:01.966
with a regulatory
authority because they

00:44:01.966 --> 00:44:03.180
didn't do a risk assessment.

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:05.550
Now, again, this doesn't
mean that everyone

00:44:05.550 --> 00:44:07.430
is beholden to a
regulator or anyone

00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:09.240
deals with regulated data.

00:44:09.240 --> 00:44:12.900
But if you do, just
do your homework.

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:14.400
So that way, one
day, if you ever

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:15.816
do have to talk
to your regulator,

00:44:15.816 --> 00:44:18.770
you can say, yes, I
did my due diligence.

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:21.300
Here, these are the
reports that I reviewed.

00:44:21.300 --> 00:44:22.500
Here is a summary.

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:24.480
Here are the certifications
that they have.

00:44:24.480 --> 00:44:25.530
Here are white papers.

00:44:25.530 --> 00:44:28.113
Here is our customer agreement
where obligations are outlined.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:33.000
That's really a
common theme I see.

00:44:33.000 --> 00:44:35.820
And you also want to,
as I mentioned before--

00:44:35.820 --> 00:44:37.380
and again, it's
important to stress--

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:40.380
you want to think about the
type of data you're storing.

00:44:40.380 --> 00:44:42.510
Most of our customers,
our millions of customers,

00:44:42.510 --> 00:44:44.047
can store all of their data.

00:44:44.047 --> 00:44:46.380
But there might be some that
are dealing with classified

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:49.440
information, or certain corpuses
of data that might otherwise be

00:44:49.440 --> 00:44:50.970
restricted .

00:44:50.970 --> 00:44:54.810
Regulatory authorities also
like to maintain oversight.

00:44:54.810 --> 00:44:57.840
So if you're a bank and you're
regulated by your banking

00:44:57.840 --> 00:45:00.450
regulator, they have to have
the ability to audit you.

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:07.470
Years ago, when I was practicing
law in the regulated market

00:45:07.470 --> 00:45:09.870
space, I was a
financial regulator.

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:12.510
And we had to have the
right as regulators

00:45:12.510 --> 00:45:15.100
to audit our customers' records.

00:45:15.100 --> 00:45:18.000
So if you're a bank-- and we
have banks that are customers--

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:19.050
and you move to
the cloud, you have

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:21.341
to think about how you're
going to maintain that access

00:45:21.341 --> 00:45:23.404
for your regulators.

00:45:23.404 --> 00:45:25.570
Are you going to grant them
an administrator account

00:45:25.570 --> 00:45:27.310
so they can access the data?

00:45:27.310 --> 00:45:29.500
What's going to be the
process for doing this?

00:45:29.500 --> 00:45:31.500
Just think about it.

00:45:31.500 --> 00:45:34.750
The regulatory
authorities also expect

00:45:34.750 --> 00:45:39.130
that regulated
industries, like banks,

00:45:39.130 --> 00:45:41.500
have to be able to
audit us directly.

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:44.690
Audit us directly, not just
look at our audit reports.

00:45:44.690 --> 00:45:46.714
All right.

00:45:46.714 --> 00:45:48.130
And the other thing
to think about

00:45:48.130 --> 00:45:50.740
is that many regulatory
authorities already have

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:52.660
guidance on adopting cloud.

00:45:52.660 --> 00:45:55.300
So don't think that you
have to do this from scratch

00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:57.789
or you have to rely just on
everything I'm telling you.

00:45:57.789 --> 00:46:00.080
It's very possible that they
may already have guidance.

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:02.413
I know lots of countries that
already have guidance with

00:46:02.413 --> 00:46:05.300
regards to cloud adoption.

00:46:05.300 --> 00:46:08.530
So from a financial
services perspective,

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:11.890
and from an FSI perspective,
as I had mentioned,

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:15.160
we've worked very hard to
create special terms for banks,

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:17.950
for example, that give
them these types of rights.

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:21.520
It gives the customer the
right to audit us directly.

00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:25.120
It even gives their regulators
the right to audit us directly.

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:28.430
We'd prefer them to use the
third party audit reports,

00:46:28.430 --> 00:46:30.830
but it is there if they need it.

00:46:30.830 --> 00:46:32.860
They could even verify
the physical controls

00:46:32.860 --> 00:46:34.202
of our data center.

00:46:34.202 --> 00:46:36.160
We don't like to open up
our data center floor.

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:37.250
It's very private.

00:46:37.250 --> 00:46:39.110
And again, it's
a security issue.

00:46:39.110 --> 00:46:41.110
But if there must be an
audit or a verification,

00:46:41.110 --> 00:46:43.810
we can make that happen as
well for certain customers

00:46:43.810 --> 00:46:46.750
of a certain size, for example,
in the financial services

00:46:46.750 --> 00:46:48.940
industry.

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:51.300
So these are the trends,
for example, in FSI.

00:46:51.300 --> 00:46:53.820
But let's talk
about other areas.

00:46:53.820 --> 00:46:56.390
You heard me talk about the
Health Insurance Portability

00:46:56.390 --> 00:46:57.390
and Accountability Act--

00:46:57.390 --> 00:46:58.500
HIPAA.

00:46:58.500 --> 00:47:02.400
And just remember that that
involves whether or not

00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:07.470
you can store protected health
information, PHI, in the cloud.

00:47:07.470 --> 00:47:10.350
And I'm happy to say that
after many years of saying no--

00:47:10.350 --> 00:47:12.840
my saying no-- we
can now say yes, you

00:47:12.840 --> 00:47:15.180
can store PHI in the cloud.

00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:17.700
It's something that we've
worked very hard for because

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:21.750
of customer demand, market
demand in the United States.

00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:24.430
I should mention, by the way,
that this is a US framework.

00:47:24.430 --> 00:47:27.060
HIPAA does not apply
to other countries.

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:32.940
But it's very important for
customers that deal with PHI.

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:35.970
Think about a university
that wants to adopt G Suite

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.929
and they have a
research hospital.

00:47:38.929 --> 00:47:41.470
And remember I told you to think
about the types of data that

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:44.050
would be stored in the cloud?

00:47:44.050 --> 00:47:46.810
Before we agreed to
store HIPAA data,

00:47:46.810 --> 00:47:48.430
universities could
use us for all

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:50.800
of their staff, all
of their students,

00:47:50.800 --> 00:47:54.820
all of their professors,
except they couldn't store PHI.

00:47:54.820 --> 00:47:58.510
Now they can, because we will
sign a business associates

00:47:58.510 --> 00:47:59.680
agreement.

00:47:59.680 --> 00:48:01.240
We've also provided
our customers

00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:03.100
with a HIPAA
implementation guide,

00:48:03.100 --> 00:48:06.640
basically an instruction manual
on how to use G Suite if you're

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:10.210
going to store PHI data.

00:48:10.210 --> 00:48:12.640
Speaking of G Suite
for education,

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:16.510
we also take into
consideration FERPA--

00:48:16.510 --> 00:48:19.450
the US Family Educational
Rights and Privacy Act.

00:48:19.450 --> 00:48:21.250
I'm glad it's written
up there for me.

00:48:21.250 --> 00:48:24.130
This involves the
protection and restrictions

00:48:24.130 --> 00:48:26.950
on how you could
process or store

00:48:26.950 --> 00:48:31.240
student records or student
directory information.

00:48:31.240 --> 00:48:33.310
That's also something we
take into consideration

00:48:33.310 --> 00:48:37.330
to help our customers that
would like to adopt G Suite.

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:40.510
Again, these won't be applicable
to everyone in the room,

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:43.540
but just to show that
we are paying attention

00:48:43.540 --> 00:48:44.810
to this for customers.

00:48:44.810 --> 00:48:48.130
And then finally, with
the US government,

00:48:48.130 --> 00:48:50.110
you heard me talk about FedRAMP.

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:54.070
We have an authorization to
operate, an ATO, at the FIPS

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:56.410
199 Moderate Impact Level.

00:48:56.410 --> 00:48:57.940
So this allows
government agencies

00:48:57.940 --> 00:49:02.590
to utilize our services pursuant
to this FedRAMP certification.

00:49:02.590 --> 00:49:05.260
And then as long as we're
talking about governments,

00:49:05.260 --> 00:49:06.910
the United Kingdom--

00:49:06.910 --> 00:49:11.080
we subscribe to the NCSC,
National Cybersecurity Center

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:13.480
cloud security
principles, which allows

00:49:13.480 --> 00:49:16.780
for government adoption in
certain markets within Europe.

00:49:16.780 --> 00:49:20.080
And we're also featured on the
government G-Cloud marketplace

00:49:20.080 --> 00:49:22.700
through our partners.

00:49:22.700 --> 00:49:28.090
So as I mentioned, we try to
provide as broad a framework

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:30.947
as possible to apply to as
many customers as possible.

00:49:30.947 --> 00:49:32.530
And within select
markets, we can also

00:49:32.530 --> 00:49:36.100
provide more, such as Europe,
healthcare, education,

00:49:36.100 --> 00:49:37.000
and government.

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:40.487
And we're working on more and
more of these frameworks for G

00:49:40.487 --> 00:49:44.140
Suite as well as in Google
Cloud Platform, which

00:49:44.140 --> 00:49:49.140
you heard about in other
parts of this week's sessions.

00:49:49.140 --> 00:49:51.220
And yes, we do have
a number of banks

00:49:51.220 --> 00:49:53.650
that have adopted our services.

00:49:53.650 --> 00:49:55.450
And we've also
worked directly with

00:49:55.450 --> 00:49:58.340
many regulatory authorities
throughout the world.

00:49:58.340 --> 00:50:00.730
As a matter of fact,
the Dutch National Bank,

00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:03.250
which is up there in
blue, they actually

00:50:03.250 --> 00:50:05.530
provided us with
direct authorization

00:50:05.530 --> 00:50:07.720
to provide services to banks.

00:50:07.720 --> 00:50:11.560
So they've already authorized
G Suite as a provider

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:14.410
to banks in that country based
on the extensive engagement

00:50:14.410 --> 00:50:17.907
that we've undertaken
with the DNB.

00:50:17.907 --> 00:50:19.240
Now, not all regulators do that.

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:22.540
They don't verify
specific vendors.

00:50:22.540 --> 00:50:24.754
Other regulators will
issue cloud guidance

00:50:24.754 --> 00:50:26.920
that you might see in other
countries like in the UK

00:50:26.920 --> 00:50:28.147
or in Australia.

00:50:28.147 --> 00:50:29.980
But in many other cases,
we'll work directly

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:32.830
with the regulatory authorities
to provide information

00:50:32.830 --> 00:50:35.660
about our services,
get their feedback.

00:50:35.660 --> 00:50:37.784
The other thing we
do is, in many cases,

00:50:37.784 --> 00:50:39.700
remember I mentioned
that sometimes regulators

00:50:39.700 --> 00:50:41.710
will issue cloud guidance?

00:50:41.710 --> 00:50:42.490
Right?

00:50:42.490 --> 00:50:45.850
So in many cases, thankfully,
the regulators actually

00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:48.670
talk to the providers so
they know a little bit

00:50:48.670 --> 00:50:50.020
about what they're writing.

00:50:50.020 --> 00:50:51.700
So in many cases,
we'll provide guidance

00:50:51.700 --> 00:50:53.770
to regulatory authorities.

00:50:53.770 --> 00:50:55.930
And in some cases, we'll
work with regulators

00:50:55.930 --> 00:50:57.430
directly along
with our customers,

00:50:57.430 --> 00:51:02.015
depending on the
country or the market.

00:51:02.015 --> 00:51:03.640
So this is just a
small example of some

00:51:03.640 --> 00:51:05.980
of the regulators that I
have worked with directly

00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:11.060
or my peers, as well
as our customers.

00:51:11.060 --> 00:51:15.025
So let's see, what have
we talked about today?

00:51:15.025 --> 00:51:16.400
We've talked about
the importance

00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:19.130
of conducting an assessment
of your cloud provider

00:51:19.130 --> 00:51:21.350
and of G Suite.

00:51:21.350 --> 00:51:23.100
You may not be in
a regulated market.

00:51:23.100 --> 00:51:25.940
You may not be dealing
with regulated data.

00:51:25.940 --> 00:51:28.670
But you should think about it
and conduct that assessment

00:51:28.670 --> 00:51:31.010
if you are, specifically.

00:51:31.010 --> 00:51:35.120
We talked about the transparency
resources we provide.

00:51:35.120 --> 00:51:39.260
Third party audit reports,
certifications, ISO,

00:51:39.260 --> 00:51:42.170
white papers, frequently
asked questions document,

00:51:42.170 --> 00:51:44.840
online resources, to
make sure that we're

00:51:44.840 --> 00:51:48.360
doing what we say we're doing.

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:50.650
And then making sure that
we put it in writing--

00:51:50.650 --> 00:51:52.310
where to find these terms.

00:51:52.310 --> 00:51:55.259
And by the way, if you want to
see the G Suite data processing

00:51:55.259 --> 00:51:57.550
amendment, just do a search
for G Suite data processing

00:51:57.550 --> 00:51:58.736
amendment.

00:51:58.736 --> 00:52:01.916
You'll find it.

00:52:01.916 --> 00:52:03.540
And then finally, I
gave you an example

00:52:03.540 --> 00:52:06.150
of some of the most common
regulatory frameworks

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:08.670
that we've been addressing
over the last few years

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:12.600
based on our experience in
providing this to business.

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:15.000
But ultimately, the
takeaway is that we'd

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:18.390
like you to consider partnering
with Google for data protection

00:52:18.390 --> 00:52:19.560
and compliance.

00:52:19.560 --> 00:52:20.950
You know your industry.

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:21.860
We know the cloud.

00:52:21.860 --> 00:52:25.380
And together, we
can really do a lot.

00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:27.320
If you would like more
information generally,

00:52:27.320 --> 00:52:29.570
please find us on the web--

00:52:29.570 --> 00:52:32.720
google.com/gsuite.

00:52:32.720 --> 00:52:35.750
And I'd like to thank you
for listening, and can also

00:52:35.750 --> 00:52:37.970
take questions for a few
minutes, if anyone has them.

00:52:37.970 --> 00:52:39.094
Thank you very much, folks.

00:52:39.094 --> 00:52:42.980
[APPLAUSE]

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:45.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

